.\" sage-runtime(1) manpage — mdoc format
.\" Version 0.1.0
.Dd October 9, 2026
.Dt SAGE-RUNTIME 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sage-runtime
.Nd switch between local container runtimes and copy their data
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar COMMAND
.Op Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
switches Docker between Colima, Docker Desktop, and OrbStack. Each
runtime has its own images and volumes. Docker talks to the runtime
named by its context.
.Pp
.Cm use
stops the other installed runtimes unless
.Fl -keep-other
is given, then starts the selected runtime, sets Docker's context, and
records the choice in
.Pa ~/.sage-is/runtime .
The saved choice takes precedence over Docker's context when
.Nm
finds the current runtime. The
.Cm copy-volume
and
.Cm copy-image
commands move data between runtimes, and
.Cm migrate
moves a whole Mac from Docker Desktop, or OrbStack, to Colima.
.Cm convert
makes a
.Sy vz
Colima VM a
.Sy krunkit
VM on the same data disk.
.Pp
A new Colima VM uses
.Sy virtiofs
mounts with file events, half the Mac's cores, clamped to 2 through 8,
and a 100 GiB disk. On Apple Silicon it is a
.Sy krunkit
VM, the default: memory the VM frees goes back to macOS, so it gets
half the Mac's memory, clamped to 4 through 12 GiB, and containers
reach the GPU through Vulkan. A container asks for the GPU with
.Fl -device Pa /dev/dri ,
and its image needs a Mesa Venus driver patched for
.Sy krunkit ,
such as the one in ghcr.io/unsuman/fedora-vgpu-llama; stock Fedora's
mesa-vulkan-drivers fails with ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY. A
.Sy krunkit
VM has no Rosetta: linux/amd64 runs there only under QEMU emulation, so
amd64 builds belong on the build VM.
.Pp
Docker keeps its images and volumes on the Colima VM's data disk. A
.Sy krunkit
VM leaves that disk unmounted after its first boot
(abiosoft/colima#1614), and Docker then starts afresh on the VM's 20 GiB
root disk, without them. Lima's override file mends this; see
.Sx FILES .
Every command that uses Colima stops while Docker runs on the root
disk. For a
.Sy krunkit
VM it first adds the mend to the override file, so
.Ql colima restart
puts Docker back on its data disk. A VM made before Colima had a data
disk keeps Docker on its root disk, as it always has.
.Pp
On Apple Silicon the Homebrew formula installs
.Sy krunkit
from the libkrun/krun tap. Homebrew 7 installs it only after that tap is
tapped and trusted; see
.Sx EXAMPLES .
When
.Sy krunkit
is missing there,
.Nm
prints a warning with the command that installs it and falls back to
Apple's
.Sy vz
hypervisor with Rosetta. An Intel Mac uses
.Sy vz
without Rosetta. A
.Sy vz
VM keeps the memory it has touched, so it gets a third of the Mac's
memory, clamped to 4 through 8 GiB. The environment can override these
values. An existing Colima VM keeps its settings, and a
.Sy vz
VM stays
.Sy vz
until
.Cm convert
makes it
.Sy krunkit .
.Pp
The build VM is a second Colima profile,
.Sy build ,
on
.Sy vz ,
with Rosetta on Apple Silicon, for linux/amd64 and multi-arch builds.
It gets a third
of the Mac's memory, clamped to 9 through 12 GiB: AI-UI's multi-arch
release wants 8 GiB in
.Ql docker info ,
and the VM's kernel keeps part of what it is given.
.Cm build-vm
creates or starts it. It never becomes Docker's context, so everyday
work stays where it was. A build names it with
.Ev DOCKER_CONTEXT Ns = Ns Sy colima-build .
.Pp
.Cm status
reports installed and running runtimes, Docker's context, and detected
configuration problems. It checks credential helpers, buildx, buildx
builders whose saved context no longer exists, and what Docker Desktop
left behind. When
.Cm use
runs, it repairs an unusable Docker credential-helper setting, removes
plugin links into Docker.app that lead nowhere, and adds the Homebrew
Docker plugin directory to the Docker configuration when needed.
.Pp
Docker Desktop is optional and stays supported. Its credential helper,
.Sy desktop ,
wraps the Keychain helper,
.Sy osxkeychain .
With Colima in use, Docker keeps its logins with the Keychain helper
itself:
.Cm use colima
switches
.Sy credsStore
to
.Sy osxkeychain
whenever that helper exists, even with Docker Desktop installed, and
every login carries over. Docker Desktop puts its own helper back when
it is the runtime again.
.Pp
.Fl h
and
.Fl -help
print usage. The
.Cm version
command and the
.Fl -version
and
.Fl v
options print the version.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width 28n
.It Cm status
Show whether each runtime is installed or running, the volume count
for each running runtime, and which runtime is in use. A runtime whose
app or VM is up while its engine does not answer counts as a problem.
Colima, not Docker, tells whether the build VM runs. Show Docker's
context and report if it differs from the selected runtime. Also report
an unavailable credential helper, missing buildx, and buildx builders
that point at missing contexts. The context of a Colima VM that exists
counts as present: Colima removes it while the VM is stopped and makes
it again at start. Links in
.Pa ~/.docker/cli-plugins
into Docker.app that lead nowhere count with any runtime, whether the
app is gone or an update dropped the plugin.
.Pp
While another runtime is in use, also report what Docker Desktop left
behind, each with the command that mends it:
.Pa /var/run/docker.sock
linked to Docker Desktop's socket, its plugin hooks in
.Pa ~/.docker/config.json ,
links into Docker.app in
.Pa /usr/local/bin
whose names start with docker, and, with Colima in use, its
.Sy desktop
credential helper where the Keychain helper exists and, while Docker
Desktop is installed, a Login Item named
.Dq Docker Desktop ,
which opens it at login. A link in
.Pa /usr/local/bin
counts once it leads nowhere, or, on an Intel Mac, where Homebrew's
prefix is
.Pa /usr/local ,
since it keeps Homebrew's own docker from linking there. There the line
also names the Homebrew formulae to link once the links go, with
.Ql brew unlink
first, since Homebrew may count one as linked already. While Docker
Desktop stays installed as a fallback on Apple Silicon, its working
links there count as nothing: its credential helper among them keeps
its logins. Login Items come from System Events, which macOS may first
ask to let this terminal read; a question with no answer in ten seconds
counts as no Login Item.
.Nm
never changes Login Items,
.Pa /var/run/docker.sock ,
.Pa /usr/local/bin
or the plugin hooks itself. Print
.Sy "No problems found."
when none are detected.
.It Cm use Ar RUNTIME Op Fl -keep-other
Stop the other installed runtimes, then start
.Ar RUNTIME ,
set Docker's context, and save the runtime choice in
.Pa ~/.sage-is/runtime .
With
.Fl -keep-other ,
leave the others running. The build VM keeps running for its builds.
The command refuses while running containers in
.Ar RUNTIME
and in another runtime or the build VM use
.Ev SAGE_DATA .
.Pp
A first launch of Docker Desktop or OrbStack opens its setup screens in
view; later launches open in the background. Sign-in is optional. The
command waits up to three minutes for the runtime to answer; see
.Ev SAGE_RUNTIME_WAIT .
It stops another runtime whose app or VM is up, even when its engine
does not answer, since its containers may still run. An OrbStack engine
started with
.Ql orb start
runs with the app closed; it counts as up, and the command stops it with
.Ql orb stop .
.Pp
First it mends Docker's configuration; see
.Pa ~/.docker/config.json
in
.Sx FILES .
It removes each link in
.Pa ~/.docker/cli-plugins
into a Docker.app that no longer holds its target, which Docker lists
as a broken plugin, and never a link that resolves.
.It Cm copy-volume Ar NAME Oo Fl -from Ar RUNTIME Oc Oo Fl -to Ar RUNTIME Oc Oo Fl -force Oc
Copy the named volume between runtimes. By default, the destination is
the runtime in use, and the source is the first installed runtime other
than the destination. The command starts both runtimes and puts
Docker's context back where it was. When that was the default context,
which Colima leaves behind as its VM stops, the context goes to the
runtime in use.
.Pp
Stop the apps that use the volume first. The command refuses, and names
the containers, while running containers on either side use the volume,
since a database copied mid-write can come out torn. It also refuses
when the copy will not fit, with a tenth and 1 GiB to spare: in the
staging folder, in the destination VM, and on the Mac disk that holds
the VM's disk image, which the VM cannot see: Colima's
.Pa _disks
folder, Docker Desktop's data folder (its
.Sy DataFolder
setting) with
.Pa Docker.raw ,
else the home folder's disk, where Docker Desktop and OrbStack keep
theirs by default. When the staging folder and that disk image share a
disk, it must hold both. A
probe that fails stops the command; it never counts as an empty volume.
.Pp
If the destination already has a non-empty volume with that name, the
command refuses to copy unless
.Fl -force
is given. With
.Fl -force ,
it deletes the destination volume's contents once it has read the
source, then writes the copy. The
command checks that the source and destination have the same number of
files and folders after the copy, and records the copy in
.Pa ~/.sage-is/migrated .
A copy that fails or is interrupted removes its staged file and leaves
no half-made volume: a volume the copy created goes, and one that was
empty, or whose data
.Fl -force
was replacing, is left empty, even when the clear itself fails partway.
.It Cm copy-image Ar REF Oo Fl -from Ar RUNTIME Oc Oo Fl -to Ar RUNTIME Oc
Copy an image between runtimes using Docker's save and load operations,
through a file in the staging folder. For Colima, the save or load runs
inside the VM, which reads the file through its mount of the home
folder: a large stream through Colima's socket can stall. Like
.Cm copy-volume ,
it refuses an image that will not fit, by the size Docker gives for
it. The default source and destination are the same as for
.Cm copy-volume .
.It Cm migrate Oo Fl -from Ar RUNTIME Oc Oo Fl -images Oc Oo Fl -dry-run Oc
Move this Mac to Colima. Copy every named volume from
.Ar RUNTIME ,
which defaults to docker-desktop, into Colima, then run
.Cm use colima .
Anonymous volumes and buildx caches stay behind. The command names each
anonymous volume that a container still uses, since on Colima that
container starts with a new, empty one. Stop your apps first:
before it copies anything, the command refuses while running containers
use any of the volumes in
.Ar RUNTIME ,
or, in Colima, any it would copy.
.Pp
A volume Colima already holds with data is never copied over. It is
skipped when the source's is empty, or when
.Pa ~/.sage-is/migrated
records a copy from
.Ar RUNTIME
and the source's file count and size have not changed since. Only such
a record vouches for Colima's copy: as many files, even the same size,
may hold other data, such as a database from a trial run or a copy cut
off inside its last file. For any other volume the command prints both
sides' file counts and sizes and the line that replaces Colima's copy:
.Cm copy-volume Ar NAME Fl -from Ar RUNTIME Fl -to Cm colima Fl -force .
While any such volume remains, the command copies the rest but does not
switch: replace each one, or keep Colima's copies and run
.Cm use colima .
.Pp
With
.Fl -images ,
also copy every tagged image Colima lacks. An image with no tag is
skipped, and an image that will not fit or fails to copy is reported
at the end; neither stops the switch. With
.Fl -dry-run ,
start nothing and copy nothing: say which runtime a real run would
start and which Colima VM it would create, and list what the running
side shows. While Colima is not running, nothing is checked against it,
so the list can name volumes and images that Colima already holds.
.Ar RUNTIME
keeps its own copy, so
.Cm use
switches back. Containers stay behind; each app's start command makes
them anew.
.It Cm convert Oo Fl -dry-run Oc Oo Fl -yes Oc
Make the everyday Colima VM, a
.Sy vz
VM made before
.Sy krunkit
was the default, a
.Sy krunkit
VM on the same data disk, with every image and volume. Without
.Fl -yes ,
or with
.Fl -dry-run ,
print the plan and change nothing. The plan names the old and new sizes,
the mounts that come across, the running containers, and the ones the
command starts again by hand. It starts nothing, so it reads only what
already runs.
.Pp
The new VM keeps the old one's memory, CPUs, disk size and mounts.
.Ev SAGE_RUNTIME_MEMORY
and
.Ev SAGE_RUNTIME_CPU
override the first two.
.Ql colima delete
takes the VM's root disk, so the command refuses unless the VM has a data
disk and shows Docker's data on it: Docker's root folder is
.Pa /var/lib/docker ,
not a
.Sy data-root
from the VM's Colima settings, and both that folder and
.Pa /var/lib/containerd ,
which holds images in Docker 29's containerd store, sit on the data
disk. The delete takes, too, a folder of the root disk that a container
binds or a volume is bound to, such as
.Pa /srv/pg
for
.Ql docker run -v /srv/pg:... ,
which Docker makes inside the VM. The volume names stay the same, so the
comparison could not see the loss: the command refuses and names each
one. The system's own folders, such as
.Pa /etc ,
.Pa /usr
and
.Pa /var/log ,
do not count. It also refuses, before it stops anything, when Lima's
override file cannot take both
.Sy krunkit
settings, when the VM's mounts hold a line it cannot carry across, and
while running containers in Colima and in another runtime or the build
VM use
.Ev SAGE_DATA .
It carries a mount's
.Sy location ,
then its
.Sy mountPoint ,
a full path, and
.Sy writable ,
as
.Fl -mount Ar PATH Ns Op : Ns Ar MOUNTPOINT Ns Op :w ;
another key, an item that does not start with its location, or a
flow-style list keeps the VM as it is.
.Pp
With
.Fl -yes ,
the command adds the
.Sy krunkit
settings to Lima's override file and starts a stopped VM. It records the
VM's size, mounts, volume names and image IDs in
.Pa ~/.sage-is/convert .
It stops the VM and runs
.Ql colima delete --force ,
never with
.Fl -data ,
so the data disk stays. It makes the
.Sy krunkit
VM on that disk and compares its volumes and images with the record.
Docker brings back the containers whose restart policy is always or
unless-stopped. The command starts the others that ran, and stops a VM
that was stopped. Containers started with
.Fl -rm
go as the VM stops, as they would at any stop, and Docker removes their
anonymous volumes with them; the plan names them, and the comparison
leaves those volumes out.
.Pp
.Ql colima delete
also removes the VM's Colima settings,
.Pa ~/.colima/default/colima.yaml .
The size and mounts come across. A dated copy of the file in
.Pa ~/.sage-is
keeps the rest, such as Docker's daemon settings.
.Pp
When anything is missing, or the run stops after the delete, the command
prints the way on and the way back. Mend the cause and run
.Cm convert Fl -yes
again: it finishes from the record. Or delete the
.Sy krunkit
VM and start a
.Sy vz
VM on the same disk with the printed command.
.It Cm build-vm Op Fl -stop
Create or start the build VM; Docker's context stays where it was. With
.Fl -stop ,
stop it whenever Colima reports it running, even when its engine does
not answer. Stop it after a build, since a
.Sy vz
VM keeps the memory a build touched.
.Pp
Restart policies bring the build VM's containers back as it starts. The
command refuses while running containers in it and in a runtime use
.Ev SAGE_DATA :
a Sprig registry on each would write there from two VMs and claim port
5000 twice. It stops the build VM again when it started it.
.It Cm version
Print the version number.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width 24n
.It Ev SAGE_RUNTIME_MEMORY
Memory in GiB for a newly created Colima VM. When unset, use half of
physical memory, clamped to 4 through 12 GiB, for a
.Sy krunkit
VM; a third, clamped to 4 through 8 GiB, for a
.Sy vz
VM; and a third, clamped to 9 through 12 GiB, for the build VM.
.Cm convert
keeps the old VM's memory when it is unset.
.It Ev SAGE_RUNTIME_CPU
Number of CPUs for a newly created Colima VM. When unset, use half the
Mac's cores, clamped to 2 through 8.
.Cm convert
keeps the old VM's CPUs when it is unset.
.It Ev SAGE_RUNTIME_DISK
Disk size in GiB for a newly created Colima VM. When unset, use 100.
.Cm convert
ignores it and keeps the size of the data disk.
.It Ev SAGE_RUNTIME_WAIT
Seconds to wait for a runtime to answer after starting it. When unset,
use 180. Each question to an engine, such as
.Ql docker info ,
gets ten seconds; a half-started engine once held one open for ten
minutes.
.It Ev DOCKER_CONTEXT , Ev DOCKER_HOST , Ev COLIMA_PROFILE
Ignored.
.Nm
reads, restores and sets Docker's saved context, the one new shells
use, and names a context on every engine call. Colima runs without
them too, so stopping the build VM from a shell that exports
.Ev DOCKER_CONTEXT Ns = Ns Sy colima-build
leaves Docker's context alone, and
.Cm use
starts and stops the everyday VM even where
.Ev COLIMA_PROFILE
names another.
.It Ev HOMEBREW_PREFIX
Homebrew prefix used to find Docker CLI plugins in
.Pa lib/docker/cli-plugins .
When unset,
.Ql brew --prefix
names it:
.Pa /opt/homebrew
on Apple Silicon,
.Pa /usr/local
on Intel.
.It Ev DOCKER_CONFIG
Docker configuration directory. When unset, the script uses
.Pa ~/.docker .
.It Ev SAGE_DATA
Folder both runtimes mount, such as the Sprig registry. Default
.Pa ~/SageData .
Two runtimes writing there at once lose data with no error, so
.Cm use
stops the other runtimes before starting one. The build VM keeps
running for its builds, so
.Cm use ,
.Cm copy-volume ,
.Cm copy-image ,
.Cm migrate ,
.Cm convert
and
.Cm build-vm
refuse while running containers in two runtimes, or in the build VM and
a runtime, use it.
.It Ev COLIMA_HOME
Colima's own folder. When unset,
.Pa ~/.colima ,
or
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/colima
while
.Pa ~/.colima
is missing, as Colima itself finds it.
.It Ev LIMA_HOME
Lima's folder for Colima's VMs, which holds Lima's override file and
the VMs' disks. When unset,
.Pa _lima
in Colima's folder.
.It Ev APPLICATIONS_DIR
Directory used to find Docker.app and OrbStack.app. When unset, the
script uses
.Pa /Applications .
.It Ev SYSTEM_SOCKET , Ev SYSTEM_BIN
Where
.Cm status
looks for Docker Desktop's socket link and its tool links. When unset,
.Pa /var/run/docker.sock
and
.Pa /usr/local/bin .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width 36n
.It Pa ~/.sage-is/runtime
Saved runtime choice. Its value takes precedence over Docker's current
context when the script determines which runtime is in use.
.It Pa ~/.sage-is/migrated
One line per verified copy: the volume, the source and destination
runtimes, and the source's file count and size. A copy removes its line
before it changes the destination and writes it once the counts match,
so a copy that stopped halfway has none.
.It Pa ~/.sage-is/convert
What
.Cm convert
keeps across the VM's delete: its size and mounts, the copy of its
settings, its volume names and image IDs, the containers to start by
hand, and whether it was stopped. A run cut short after the delete leaves
the file, and the next run finishes from it.
.It Pa ~/.sage-is/colima-default.YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.yaml
The copy of the
.Sy vz
VM's Colima settings that
.Cm convert
makes before
.Ql colima delete
removes them.
.It Pa ~/.sage-is/staging
The file each copy passes through, one copy at a time. The file goes
when its copy ends, whether it finished, failed or was interrupted.
Each copy sets the folder to mode 700: a staged file holds a whole
volume or image, and another account on the Mac can read the home
folder.
.It Pa ~/.docker/config.json
Docker configuration read and updated by
.Cm use .
The command can repair an unavailable credential helper, hand Docker
Desktop's
.Sy desktop
helper to
.Sy osxkeychain
with Colima in use, and register the Homebrew plugin directory here.
.It Pa ~/.docker/config.json.YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.bak
Dated backup made before
.Cm use
changes the credential-helper setting or adds the plugin directory.
.It Pa ~/.colima/_lima/_config/override.yaml
Lima settings for every Colima VM, in Lima's folder; see
.Ev LIMA_HOME .
Before it creates a
.Sy krunkit
VM,
.Nm
adds two settings here, each under a comment naming its issue, and
keeps whatever the file held. The first is
.Sy mountType: virtiofs ,
unless the file already sets a mount type: Colima 0.10.3 asks Lima for
9p for a
.Sy krunkit
VM, and Lima 2.2 refuses it (abiosoft/colima#1607).
.Sy vz
and
.Sy krunkit
VMs both use virtiofs, but a QEMU Colima profile on the same Mac will
not start while the line is there. The second is a provision step that
mounts each Lima disk by its label at boot. A
.Sy krunkit
VM puts its data disk on vdc, where Lima 2.2 looks for vdb, so the disk
stays unmounted after the VM's first boot (abiosoft/colima#1614). A
.Sy vz
VM has its disk mounted already, and the step passes it by. When the
file has its own provision list,
.Nm
leaves it alone and prints the step to add to that list. On a
.Sy krunkit
VM,
.Ql colima ssh \-\- findmnt /var/lib/docker
then shows /dev/vdc1.
.It Pa ~/.docker/buildx/instances
Buildx builder records checked by
.Cm status
for endpoints that do not match an available Docker context.
.It Pa lib/sage-runtime.sh
The runtime code
.Nm
sources from beside itself, in a checkout or in the formula's
.Pa libexec
folder. A link to
.Nm ,
such as one in
.Pa ~/bin ,
finds it beside the file the link leads to. Without it,
.Nm
stops and asks for
.Ql brew reinstall sage-runtime ,
or a checkout of the tap.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Move from Docker Desktop to Colima
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-runtime migrate --dry-run
sage-runtime migrate
.Ed
.Ss Switch to Colima
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-runtime use colima
.Ed
.Ss Make a vz Colima VM krunkit, after the plan
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-runtime convert
sage-runtime convert --yes
.Ed
.Ss Tap and trust libkrun/krun, then install krunkit
.Bd -literal -offset indent
brew tap libkrun/krun && brew trust libkrun/krun && brew install krunkit
.Ed
.Ss Build an amd64 image on the build VM, in an AI-UI checkout
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-runtime build-vm
DOCKER_CONTEXT=colima-build make it_build_amd64
sage-runtime build-vm --stop
.Ed
.Ss Copy the trellis-data volume from Docker Desktop
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-runtime copy-volume trellis-data --from docker-desktop --to colima
.Ed
.Ss Copy an image
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-runtime copy-image alpine:3 --from docker-desktop --to colima
.Ed
.Ss Switch back to Docker Desktop
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sage-runtime use docker-desktop
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success and 1 on any error.
.Cm status
exits 0 even when it reports problems. A signal ends
.Nm
with 128 plus the signal's number, such as 130 for Ctrl-C.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr docker 1
.Pp
These have no manual pages; run each with
.Fl -help :
.Sy colima ,
.Sy ai-ui
and
.Sy trellis-crm .
For
.Sy krunkit ,
see
.Lk https://github.com/libkrun/krunkit .
